<!-- The notification mark: the same cat R the launcher icon uses, sized to
     fill the slot a status bar gives it.

     A notification small icon is an alpha mask. The system tints it with the
     bar's own foreground colour, so only the shape survives: it is drawn in
     white because the value is ignored, and any colour or gradient here would
     be flattened to the same silhouette anyway.

     No <group> transform. An earlier revision wrapped the artwork in
     translate+scale, which is valid VectorDrawable and renders in a browser,
     but the notification path does not use the same renderer: the system
     rasterises this drawable through its own icon pipeline. The 0.099311
     scale put every coordinate far outside the 24-unit viewport before the
     matrix applied, and on a device the icon came out unchanged - the
     silhouette never reached the bar. The transform is baked into the path
     data instead: every coordinate is absolute and already inside 0..24, so
     there is nothing left to interpret.

     Size: the drawn bbox is 19.30 x 21.80dp, centred on (12, 12). Android
     clips a notification icon to a 22dp safe area, so this fills it with
     1.10dp of margin top and bottom (and 2.35dp left and right, because the
     glyph is narrower than it is tall). Pushing it to a full 22dp left the
     bottom at 0.999dp, close enough to the clip to shave the foot of the leg
     on some renderers, so it stops just short. -->
